SM(463,{"n":"Acts 11","a":"Fr Stephen De Young Archives/Acts 11.mp3","d":3136.0,"s":[[0.16,"Come and study Holy Scriptures with us as Father Stephen DeYoung teaches verse by verse on the podcast The Whole Counsel of God."],[8.08,"Father Stephen is a former Protestant pastor and Bible teacher in the Reformed Church, but now an Orthodox priest serving at Archangel Gabriel Orthodox Church in Lafayette, Louisiana."],[20.8,"Okay. So we'll go ahead and get started here in uh just a minute."],[25.84,"And when we get started, uh we're gonna pick up at the beginning of Acts the Apostles Chapter 11."],[32.4,"Uh, like I always say, uh by the time people are hearing this on the internet, the first uh Bible study of the book of Acts will be up there for you to go back and listen to uh for the introduction to the book uh and all of that."],[48.0,"Uh in terms of today, I'm gonna be helped a little in my recap because as we begin in chapter 11, Saint Peter is going to sort of recap what happened in chapter 10."],[59.2,"So as we're reading that, we'll uh get caught back up by him."],[65.96,"But uh just in general, to sort of get us back up to speed with where we were."],[71.4,"Uh we had read about uh at the beginning of uh the book of Acts, uh Saint Luke uh sort of retold the story of Christ's ascension into heaven that he had ended his gospel with."],[86.84,"Uh, and then we had read about uh the coming of the Holy Spirit upon the disciples gathered in Jerusalem, and uh the beginnings of through their pre the apostles' preaching, the conversions and the beginnings of the church and their life in Jerusalem."],[103.0,"Uh that led up to uh the death of uh Saint Stephen, uh, whose uh relics were found today many centuries ago, but which is celebrated today."],[116.48,"Uh the death of Saint Stephen at the hands of a mob, but really at the hands of the religious leaders of Jerusalem who sort of incited that mob, uh, including one Saul of Tarsus."],[129.12,"Uh that uh that the death of uh Saint Stephen as the first martyr uh had sort of touched off a wave of persecution that had scattered a lot of the Christians who had been living in Jerusalem to other places, and so that persecution, though it was intended to sort of get rid of Christianity, had ended up sort. of spreading it all over the place because those people, as they went to other cities, had preached the gospel of Christ"],[156.52,"And so now there are it was not only the sort of nascent Christian community in Jerusalem, which is still there, albeit a little smaller, but there are now nascent Christian communities sort of all over the place."],[169.32,"Notably, as we're going to see in chapter 11 in Antioch, where it's continuing to grow."],[174.28,"But we saw that there were Christians in Damascus, there were Christians in Samaria, there were Christian, there's even at least one Christian on his way back to Ethiopia after St."],[184.36,"Philip preached to him."],[186.44,"And so it is now spreading sort of all over the world."],[189.64,"And we read after that of the conversion"],[193.4,"We talked about whether conversion is the right word for it, but uh uh the repentance at least and the calling of uh Saul of Tarsus, who is going to become Saint Paul, um, as he uh set out to persecute further the Christian community in Damascus and then ended up joining the Christian community in Damascus."],[214.8,"Uh he now has our story has turned back to Saint Peter here for a little bit, but we're gonna get back to Saint Paul shortly uh and what he's been doing."],[224.88,"Uh and then uh most recently in chapter 10, we read about uh this incident involving uh Cornelius the Centurion, a Roman Centurion who had been sort of a friend of the Jewish community, um and and had become we we talked at that point I'll I'll talk about it a little again here, only because it's going to continue to be important as we go forward in the book of Acts, so it bears repeating."],[269.0,"Cornelius, uh, we're told had actually stopped worshiping those other gods and was only worshiping and serving the God of Israel."],[278.48,"And so this group, though, though they were interested in Judaism, though they had access to the Jewish scriptures and Greek, and they would give money to the synagogues and they would, in their personal piety, worship the God of Israel, they weren't really ready to go the whole way and become Jewish."],[298.22,"It'd be, if they were males, be circumcised and bring their families and start following all of the food regulations and all of these other regulations and things in order to fully become Jewish."],[310.3,"And so they were sort of at theing Jewish Centurion, be a Robocenturion, if he had decided to become, he would have had to give up his position, his you know, um, and so it's no wonder that he sort of remained at the fringe of the Jewish community uh for those reasons, um, at least while his military career was still going on."],[337.38,"Uh, but uh he received a vision uh during his prayers telling him to call for Saint Peter, uh, who was in Joppa at the time."],[345.86,"Saint Peter went and uh really bore witness to the fact that uh the Holy Spirit came upon Cornelius and his family and the people of his household, despite them still being Gentiles and not having done any of these other things of circumcision or ritual washings or anything that were required to become Jewish."],[366.42,"The Holy Spirit had fallen upon them the same way it had fallen upon he and the the other disciples, and so he baptized them them. and uh made what will soon be called Christians"],[380.38,"So is this the first Gentile?"],[383.5,"Well, the Ethiopian eunuch was baptized, and as an Ethiopian was a was a Gentile."],[392.46,"But this is um there was a community of Ethiopian Jews already at this point in history."],[403.02,"Uh so this is another step removed."],[407.58,"Uh a Roman, Romans were sort of the Gentiles of all Gentiles."],[412.14,"They were the they were the ones who were actively oppressing, you know, the Judean people."],[416.22,"This one's even serving in the military, you know, he's sort of in government. service"],[421.14,"So this is sort of the most extreme case we've seen of someone who is about as far outside of their normative ideas as possible."],[432.98,"And that's where we're going to pick up here in chapter 11, because uh after these baptisms, uh St."],[440.66,"Peter is going to return to Jerusalem, and uh not everyone is going to have the same opinion of what he just did that he did."],[449.94,"Uh so unless there are any other questions or comments left over from before, we'll go ahead and get started here in uh Acts chapter 11, verse 1."],[461.3,"Now the apostles and brethren who are in Judea heard that the Gentiles had also received the Word of God."],[469.74,"So we'll pause there for a second to write a couple things."],[472.22,"The reason it says apostles and brethren is it means sort of the whole community."],[477.18,"There's the apostles who are the leaders, and then the brethren, which means the rest of the community."],[498.3,"When it says brethren, that doesn't just mean the men."],[501.56,"That means the whole community."],[503.36,"Um in Hebrew and in Aramaic, and in Greek, if you have a group that's mixed men and women, you use masculine pronouns."],[515.4,"So that covers everybody."],[517.48,"Um, so that they're referring to the whole community."],[520.44,"Uh, it is worth noting though that the terms that are being used to describe the community are already familial terms."],[530.76,"Meaning the people who are members of the community, even though they aren't brought blood relatives, consider themselves are are referring to them each other as brothers and sisters."],[541.05,"And this is gonna be where, and we're gonna see when we get into uh 1 Corinthians and some of the other uh epistles later in the New Testament, where we'll see the apostles and other leaders in the church uh referring to themselves as fathers and the people in the church who they're writing to as their children."],[560.45,"Um so this understanding of uh the Christian community is sort of the household of God, and that the the uh the leadership are like fathers or like parents, and the the members of the community are brothers and sisters, is already present at this early point."],[581.27,"Um I I put such an emphasis on that because a lot of our Protestant friends really don't like clergy being called father, for example."],[592.79,"Um, but this is where that comes from."],[595.47,"Uh-huh. It comes from this. idea that that we in the church together are a family"],[600.91,"And so the leaders are like fathers and the the people are like their children, and to each other they're brothers and sisters in the Lord."],[610.49,"This family language."],[613.45,"Brother and sister, yeah."],[618.49,"Uh and then secondly, remember uh when it talks about uh the Gentiles had also received the word of God."],[626.25,"We're so used in our used to in our modern world when we hear word of God to think this, to think the Bible."],[634.19,"But that's pretty clearly not what they're talking about here, because there wasn't anything in there that St."],[638.43,"Peter gave Cornelius a Bible or a copy of the Hebrew scriptures or anything like that."],[645.12,"He'd received the word of the word of God here is referring to to Christ and to the gospel."],[651.42,"Because the gospel is Christ himself."],[655.19,"So the Gentiles also have received Christ, have received the message of Christ and so have received Christ."],[663.67,"Not just Jewish people are are receiving Christ."],[670.31,"And you know, we we underestimate how important this is now"],[675.96,"Um, it wasn't that long ago that even in this country there would have been questions whether people of certain ethnicities and racial backgrounds could really receive Christ."],[688.84,"You know, um, unfortunately."],[692.04,"But, you know, in our era, the idea that somebody because they're of a certain ethnicity couldn't it seems absurd to us."],[700.36,"Uh it wasn't so absurd at this time."],[703.86,"Because I mean, Jesus was the Jewish Messiah."],[708.83,"He was the king of the Jews."],[710.91,"Um, and so the shift in understanding, we we we've seen already how uh Saint Luke has been showing us, you know, sort of the reversing of the Tower of Babel in in the day of Pentecost and this kind of thing."],[726.15,"And God is now calling God, is God is the of the whole world, Christ the Lord of all the earth, and it's now calling these other nations back."],[727.63,"the But that wasn't totally apparent to people living at this time."],[737.13,"So Jesus wasn't the Roman Messiah."],[739.63,"He was the Jewish Messiah."],[741.79,"He's the one who came to answer these promises that were made to to the Jewish people."],[746.99,"No promises were made to the Romans, in their minds, in their minds."],[751.81,"And so when they hear that the Gentiles also have received the Word of God."],[758.97,"Their initial response isn't to just all rejoice and be happy."],[762.21,"Oh, isn't this wonderful?"],[763.83,"They have some questions."],[765.19,"Uh-hhu. It's like, why?"],[766.24,"How does this work?"],[770.41,"And when Peter came up to Jerusalem, those of the circumcision contended with him saying, You went into uncircumcised men and ate with them."],[781.45,"So they're not even at the whole becoming Christians part."],[788.49,"They're not even up to the baptism part."],[790.33,"They're like, why did you go into that house?"],[793.51,"Why did you sit down and eat with them?"],[796.63,"Now again, this seems uh probably in our terms, it probably in our modern terms, it seems super racist."],[804.28,"Like you wouldn't even go into someone's house, you know, or or eat food with them."],[809.64,"Um but hospitality had a much more important role in the ancient world uh than it necessarily does for us today, especially in American culture."],[823.36,"Some other cultures in the world, other the American culture still have a very strong emphasis on hospitality."],[830.64,"In the in the United States today, families don't even eat dinner together half the time, let alone have other people over to come and eat with them."],[843.92,"But so that this the idea of being a guest in someone's home and especially of sitting down at table to eat with them, uh, what meant that you were sort of in fellowship and in communion with them."],[856.5,"That you were uniting yourself to them."],[858.86,"And so if you invited a person to come do that with you, or if you went and did that with a person who was problematic, you were seen as sort of joining yourself to this person."],[872.79,"So if someone had a bad reputation."],[875.73,"So we and you went and you ate with them."],[878.93,"And that's exactly why Jesus gets so much heat from the people from the Pharisees in particular for going and eating with tax collectors and sinners."],[889.25,"It's not just that, oh, tax collectors and sinners are filthy or something."],[894.21,"It's that you know, you go and you sit and you eat with them, you know, how can you have fellowship with this person? who is doing these wicked, these wicked and evil things"],[905.69,"It's it's sort of like if if you you know saw a photo of someone, you know, chatting with Hitler."],[912.17,"Uh-huh. You know."],[915.05,"Whoa! You know, sort of like does that mean you agree with him?"],[920.33,"Does that mean you, you know?"],[922.09,"Um, so we we saw a little, but but you know, this is why they're asking him this."],[928.33,"It's not just that they're racist against Gentiles and think they're filthy."],[932.65,"Okay. Because you can go back and you can read the the Torah."],[937.85,"There's there's nothing in there saying that Gentiles are in and of themselves unclean."],[942.81,"Now they may do things that make themselves unclean."],[946.23,"They go to an idol's temple, they go and do pagan ritual."],[949.03,"That makes them unclean."],[950.47,"But just being a Gentile doesn't make you unclean."],[954.24,"Uh and there's all kinds of regulation about that."],[956.8,"But it's the fact that St."],[957.92,"Peter went in with this Roman centurion and sat down and ate with him and treated him like he was, you know, an equal when this person is a pagan and this person is this and that, the other in the minds of the people who are who are coming to him."],[975.44,"Yeah. So we need to we need to sort of understand that that this isn't just sort of Jewish people are racist against everyone else."],[983.28,"That this they see sitting down and eating with someone."],[986.88,"The place where that's most preserved in the church is actually in the Eucharist, is in communion."],[992.36,"We don't give communion to just anybody who walks in off the street."],[996.5,"Because by sharing communion together, by coming to the chalice together, we're saying that we have one faith and one belief, and we're united together in Christ."],[1004.83,"And so they see eating with essentially as a syllabus."],[1010.03,"You're saying that you and he are in it together."],[1014.51,"So what does that say about you?"],[1021.79,"But Peter explained it to them in order from the beginning, saying, I was in the city of Joppa praying, and in a trance I saw a vision, an object descending like a great sheet let down from heaven by four corners, and it came to me."],[1038.55,"Remember, this is we've already read about this when it happened, but remember that you saw sort of like an upside-down parachute."],[1045.24,"With ropes, and it came down out of heaven."],[1049.64,"When I observed it intently and considered, I saw four-footed animals of the earth, wild beasts, creeping things, and birds of the air."],[1059.16,"Creeping things there is actually the word herpetone."],[1062.04,"It means lizards. all the unclean animals you're not supposed to eat"],[1070.32,"And I heard a voice saying to me, Rise, Peter, kill and eat."],[1074.44,"And we mentioned last time, you notice cook isn't in there anywhere."],[1077.8,"Yeah So it's telling him to just let grab onto this stuff, kill it, and start eating."],[1083.6,"But I said, Not so, Lord, for nothing common or unclean has at any time entered my mouth."],[1089.28,"But the voice answered me again from heaven."],[1091.6,"What God has cleansed, you must not call common."],[1097.92,"Uh last time that it's not don't call these things unclean."],[1105.36,"It's what God has cleansed, what God has made clean, do not call unclean."],[1110.8,"Meaning these things were at one point unclean."],[1114.72,"But now I have cleansed them, and so now, don't you dare."],[1122.8,"That cleansing, do, does thates that refer to specifically these creatures in the vision or that mean now that Christ has cleansed the creation or something like that?"],[1136.76,"The latter. When you read uh Leviticus, and at some point after we finish Revelation, we're gonna loop around and I'll do the Pedateuch again."],[1149.56,"Um, it won't be that long."],[1152.36,"Um, but yeah."],[1153.96,"Um Leviticus, the the food laws and the cleanliness regulations are aimed at the idea that there is in in the wilderness, when they're wandering in the wilderness, there's the camp."],[1167.48,"The center of the camp is the tabernacle where God lives with them."],[1169.16,"And there's the camp, and then there's the outside world"],[1176.32,"And the outside world is corrupted by sin."],[1181.52,"The outside world is under the power of evil spiritual forces."],[1187.44,"You see this, for example, in Leviticus 16 in the Day of Atonement ritual."],[1192.34,"They take the two goats, and the one goat, Aaron lays his hands upon it and puts the sins of the people on it."],[1200.26,"And then they send it out of the camp into the wilderness."],[1203.5,"To Azazel. Azazel is one of these demonic spirits."],[1209.64,"That's out there in the world, that's in control of the world."],[1212.2,"But the camp is the place where God. is"],[1214.32,"And so because that's the place where God is, they have to keep the camp clean and pure."],[1220.58,"And set apart and holy."],[1223.31,"So that God can continue to dwell there with them."],[1227.07,"And what's said about the camp then becomes true when they come into the land, that becomes true of the land."],[1232.11,"God lives in the temple in the midst of the land."],[1234.67,"And what happens when they become corrupt and sinful, and just like the rest of the world, is God has to leave the temple and then the temple's destroyed."],[1242.45,"Because God can't stay there anymore because it's become full of sin and wickedness."],[1246.85,"And he can't be where sin is."],[1251.85,"So the the food laws for the animals, you'll know the animals that you're allowed to eat are all domesticated animals that you would have in the camp."],[1261.85,"And the things you're not allowed to eat is all the wild animals and stuff."],[1265.29,"You're not allowed to eat stuff that you find dead."],[1268.17,"You're not allowed to eat um carrying birds, you're not allowed to eat, you know, sort of these things that live out in the wilderness."],[1276.17,"It's just these domesticated animals that live in the camp."],[1278.73,"And when you kill them, you have to kill them in a certain way, and you drain the blood and you offer a portion of it to God as a sacrifice, you know, and then you eat the rest, you know, with your with your family."],[1294.97,"The halal rules, yeah."],[1299.37,"Yeah. Yeah, they're based on the Jewish."],[1301.69,"' Thats why they won't eat pork either."],[1302.41,"Hala means like that"],[1303.33,"Yeah. Halal, halal meat."],[1308.93,"Halal. And so all of those rules are aimed at preserving this holy space in the midst of the world that's under the control of these dark powers and it is tainted by sin."],[1322.93,"Well, Christ in his crucifixion and resurrection doesn't just cleanse Judea."],[1331.22,"Of their sins."],[1333.3,"He cleanses the whole world, both of sin."],[1338.8,"And he defeats those dark spiritual forces."],[1343.12,"So they're no longer in control of the world."],[1345.44,"Christ is the Lord. of all the earth"],[1348.52,"And so that means that all of these animals now are not under the dominion of these dark spiritual forces out in the wilderness."],[1357.8,"They're now under Christ's dominion."],[1360.28,"And more importantly, where this image is going is all of the people of the world are no longer under the control of these dark forces."],[1371.72,"But are under Christ's lordship."],[1377.96,"Not just Israel as this little camp in the midst of the midst of the world."],[1384.12,"So that's what this vision, that's the idea that this vision is aiming at."],[1387.56,"And St. Paul is going to talk about this a lot more when we get into his epistles."],[1390.84,"He's going to talk about, like in Colossians, how in former days they were sort of enslaved by the elemental powers of the earth."],[1397.04,"You know, they were worshiping these gods."],[1399.2,"And St. Paul is going to quote the Psalm and say, all the gods of the nations are demons."],[1405.28,"So for Saint Paul, you know, the Roman gods, the Greek gods, the Egyptian gods that they're the Indian gods that they're worshiping, it isn't that he comes and says, Oh, they don't exist."],[1418.72,"He says, No, these are evil spiritual forces who have enslaved you, who have led you astray, who have been leading you to destruction."],[1428.26,"And they're not really, they're not worthy of being called gods."],[1432.99,"Christ is the one who is, and he's set you free from. them by defeating them so that you can worship him"],[1439.91,"Um I've often heard the phrase of one way or another, that uh Satan is the Lord of this world."],[1449.11,"That ends with Christ, so it's no longer true, it was true."],[1454.64,"Um and and this um we saw well, we saw in the gospel of of John."],[1461.09,"As he's as he's giving that final discourse to his disciples, as he's preparing to go to his death, he says, Now is the prince of this world judged."],[1469.97,"Uh-huh. I remember that."],[1472.43,"And uh, yeah, and so that's that's no longer true."],[1475.95,"And this is what uh in the book of Revelation, this is what St."],[1479.59,"John is going to describe as this sort of as this binding of Satan."],[1484.81,"Where he's bound and thrown into a pit."],[1486.65,"Christ reigns with the saints for this long period of time, which is metaphorically a thousand years, not a literal thousand years, but for this period of time."],[1496.25,"Then at the end, the devil is sort of let loose for this last sort of frenzied attack before Christ returned in his ultimate defeat."],[1508.73,"And the reason for that span of time is to allow for as many people, many humans, for as many people as possible to repent and find salvation."],[1522.25,"And God in his mercy. has extended that period of time"],[1531.97,"Okay. So God has cle that's what is being referred to as what God has cleansed."],[1538.38,"So these animals have been cleansed."],[1539.98,"They've been brought in, they've been made part of God's dominion, his reign."],[1544.94,"This is this is before we continue one last note on this."],[1549.58,"We we translate uh the word uh Vasileia as uh kingdom."],[1556.7,"And the problem with that word in English is that it makes us think of a like a thing, like a place."],[1563.96,"You say, blessed is the kingdom at the beginning of liturgy."],[1566.76,"We talk about kingdom come like it's somewhere else and it's gonna come here and show up"],[1567.76,"As if we're talking about a place."],[1574.08,"You know. That it's a this place."],[1576.66,"But the the word really refers to dominion or rule or reign."],[1584.1,"So we're not talking about the kingdom of God as if, again, like in the old testament, where there's this physical kingdom on earth, there's this place."],[1592.5,"We're talking about Christ's rule or his reign, which is over all the earth."],[1598.43,"And so we're we're blessing that, and we're entering into that, coming under Christ's lordship when we come together in worship."],[1608.19,"And when the gospel is spread, that extends Christ's Lordship. to these other places and to these other people"],[1617.35,"So that concept is sort of related to what we're we're talking about."],[1625.35,"So St. Peter continues."],[1626.71,"Now this was done three times, and all were drawn up again into heaven."],[1630.87,"At that very moment, three men stood before the house where I was, having been sent to me from Caesarea."],[1637.59,"Then the Spirit told me to go with them, doubting nothing."],[1641.03,"Moreover, these six brethren accompanied me, and we entered the man's house."],[1645.99,"And he told us how he had seen an angel standing in his house, who said to him, Send men to Joppa and call for Simon, whose surname is Peter, who will tell you words. by which you and all your household will be saved"],[1661.07,"So again, surname isn't the best translation, because surname makes it sound like Simon is his first name and Peter is his last name."],[1673.37,"That's when we know that Petros uh was his nickname."],[1679.93,"Sort of his nickname that Jesus gave him."],[1682.73,"Um and we talked before how it meant sort of rock or Rocky, sort of the way we use Rocky as a nickname."],[1690.81,"Um, and it's somewhat descriptive of his character, as we see him in the gospels and his personality."],[1695.77,"Uh he's a little bit of a rough and tumble guy."],[1698.57,"So um but so yes, so uh St."],[1705.01,"Peter's making the point that not only did I have this vision telling me to go do this, but Cornelius, who I was going to, had this vision, and the people from him arrived just as I was having the vision."],[1720.13,"So if you if the people who are hearing this understand St."],[1723.73,"Peter's story, they said understand that well, whatever happened there at Cornelius' house, it was pretty clearly something God wanted to happen."],[1731.17,"Uh-huh. God wanted this to happen."],[1734.13,"He wanted it to happen at this particular point in time, and he wanted these people to be involved."],[1744.77,"And as I began to speak, the Holy Spirit fell upon them, as upon us at the beginning."],[1750.01,"Then I remembered the word of the Lord, how he said, John indeed baptized with water, but you shall be baptized with the Holy Spirit."],[1757.45,"If therefore God gave them the same gift as he gave us when we believed on the Lord Jesus Christ, who was I that I could withstand God?"],[1765.85,"And we talked last time about how the fact that the normal order is reversed."],[1769.86,"Normally we're going to see in the book of Acts, people will get baptized and then they'll receive the Holy Spirit."],[1774.66,"But in this case, God didn't wait."],[1778.68,"The Holy Spirit comes upon them immediately while he's still preaching to them."],[1783.56,"Why? Well, as St."],[1785.4,"Peter says, to give him a single signal."],[1788.62,"You need to baptize these people."],[1789.34,"Don't hold off"],[1791.94,"That's the point."],[1793.56,"It's okay. And as St."],[1797.16,"Peter says, well, if they've already received the Holy Spirit, how can I tell them that they can't be baptized with water?"],[1807.25,"When they heard these things, they became silent and they glorified God, saying that God has also granted to the Gentiles repentance to life."],[1820.85,"This is uh a very good group of Jewish Christians."],[1826.29,"As we go through the rest of the New Testament, we're going to encounter some other groups of Jewish Christians who are not so understanding about the Gentiles."],[1832.45,"Uh, but these at least come around fairly quickly"],[1838.81,"Um, it's a pretty impressive story."],[1841.29,"Yeah. Well, and it's very clear."],[1844.41,"In order to say that this isn't something God wanted, you would have to basically call St."],[1848.73,"Peter a liar."],[1849.99,"Because if his if his story of how it happened is true, it's pretty clear that God wanted at least these Gentiles to be baptized."],[1859.51,"Um but notice also how they understand this."],[1864.52,"Uh it isn't that, oh, isn't it nice that Gentiles can get saved too?"],[1870.66,"It says God has also granted to the Gentiles repentance to life."],[1874.82,"So they see what happens already at this stage"],[1881.66,"They see what happens in baptism and receiving the Holy Spirit as the beginning of something, not as the end of something."],[1890.22,"Okay, now you're Christians, you're good."],[1893.5,"But they see this as the beginning of a life of repentance."],[1898.06,"And that life of repentance is going to lead to life, meaning eternal life."],[1904.06,"That's where this is headed."],[1905.82,"And so the gift that's been granted to the Gentiles is repentance and forgiveness of sins."],[1915.16,"And this provides a way for them as Jewish Christians to understand this."],[1921.88,"Because remember, their problem with the Gentiles and with St."],[1924.36,"Peter going in there is these people are involved in various kinds of wickedness."],[1930.37,"Idolatry, you know, sexual immorality, all kinds of things."],[1933.91,"The Romans, how can you be with them?"],[1936.23,"Well, if God has granted them repentance, then that's not who they are anymore."],[1943.33,"That's not who they are anymore."],[1944.85,"They now have this new identity in Christ and they're on this new path."],[1949.09,"And so that gives them a way to understand it."],[1951.49,"As I said, unfortunately, we're going to run into some other people who don't quite understand that as well."],[1960.53,"Now, those who were scattered after the persecution that arose over Stephen traveled as far as Phoenicia, Cyprus, and Antioch, preaching the word to no one but the Jews only."],[1972.97,"Okay. So they haven't had the vision Saint Peter had."],[1978.81,"So they're primarily, as they travel, just going to the Jewish communities in these different places and telling them, hey, the Messiah has come, it's Jesus of Nazareth, and preaching the gospel to them."],[1989.37,"And so these are forming into sort of more nascent Christian communities."],[1995.93,"Phoenicia is basically give or take what's now Lebanon."],[2003.77,"And Cyprus, of course, is still Cyprus."],[2007.37,"And Antioch, this Antioch is Antioch."],[2010.37,"There are a number of cities named Antioch."],[2013.25,"We're going to find that as we go through."],[2015.17,"Several of them are going to show up in the book of Acts."],[2017.89,"So there's Pisidian Antioch."],[2019.89,"There are a whole series of."],[2021.09,"And that's because Antiochus was the surname of a series of Greek rulers over the kingdom that was headquartered in Syria."],[2029.97,"And they tended to name a lot of cities after themselves and their fathers and their sons."],[2035.73,"So you end up with a whole bunch of Antiochs."],[2039.17,"For the same reason that there are a whole bunch of Caesarea's and a whole bunch of Alexandrias named after Alexander the Great."],[2046.61,"You get a whole bunch of uh a whole bunch of Antiochs."],[2049.89,"This, however, is is the primary."],[2051.97,"This is a major Roman capital of the East."],[2053.09,"And and it's on the Arontis River"],[2055.69,"The capital of that Syria."],[2060.73,"It's at the very northern edge between what's now Lebanon and what's now Turkey."],[2068.17,"No, Syria and Turkey."],[2070.41,"Yeah. Yeah. Yeah."],[2071.93,"It's in Turkey now."],[2072.97,"Yeah. Historically, it's all right."],[2075.05,"Well, historically, yeah, it was in Syria."],[2076.49,"Yeah. Well, historically, a lot of what's now Lebanon was in Syria."],[2081.69,"But uh, but yeah, it's right."],[2083.53,"Yeah. It's the third biggest city of the Roman Empire."],[2086.09,"Yeah. Yeah, at this point."],[2088.41,"At this point."],[2089.21,"And had it had the second largest Jewish community after Jerusalem at this point."],[2096.17,"Antioch. Antioch did."],[2098.09,"Antioch at its founding was about a third Jewish"],[2098.21,"Yeah. And there was a Jewish district called the Kephilaeon that was so big that when uh Titus marched in and destroyed the temple in Jerusalem, he took the decorative cherubim from the temple and had them hung suspended in the Jewish quarter in Antioch."],[2121.09,"To sort of make the point, you know, I don't want you guys getting any ideas."],[2128.61,"The way they did in Jerusalem."],[2130.69,"Um, because we could wipe this out too, kind of idea."],[2134.29,"Um, and yeah, that Jewish community in Antioch was there until the time of St."],[2140.29,"John Chrysostom and occasioned his sermons against sometimes they're called the sermons against the Jews, the sermons against the Judaizers."],[2148.57,"Um, but they're dealing with sort of conflicts between the Jewish and Christian communities there at that point."],[2153.61,"Because even in the late fourth and early fifth centuries, the Jewish community in Antioch was bigger than the Christian community in Antioch."],[2161.85,"It was a huge uh Jewish community there."],[2164.57,"But yes, it was a major city."],[2166.97,"Um that's why it gets, you know, the other two things that are mentioned there are basically regions of Phoenicia and Cyprus, whereas Antioch is a city, but it's such a big city that it you know is of note."],[2183.37,"Um but so yeah, they're going to the Jewish communities there and proclaiming Jesus at this point."],[2187.69,"and But some of them were men from Cyprus Cyrene, who, when they had come to Antioch, spoke to the Hellenists preaching the Lord Jesus."],[2200.85,"So Cyrene was in what's now North Africa."],[2209.41,"Yeah. In North Africa."],[2230.77,"Greek would have been their primary language, even though. they were Jewish because they're so far from Palestine, you know"],[2238.25,"Um and so they interface with these Hellenists."],[2241.61,"Uh these Hellenists are not Hellenistic Jews."],[2245.98,"Which is kind of obvious from context because it says they were going only to the Jews, but some here went to the Hellenists."],[2253.66,"If those were just another group of Jews, it wouldn't make a lot of sense."],[2256.78,"Uh so these are these Greeks, these Hellenists are Greeks who are God fearers, like Cornelius."],[2265.02,"So some of them who are very fluent in Greek and uh are are sort of uh acculturated, in Greek culture, they're going to these Greeks who are sort of near Judaism."],[2276.8,"In addition to the Jewish communities themselves, and telling them about Christ"],[2283.37,"And the hand of the Lord was with them, and a great number believed and turned to the Lord."],[2288.73,"Now, this is going to be important coming up because we have now, beyond just this one family of Cornelius."],[2297.07,"We now have Jewish communities that are accepting Jesus as the Messiah and becoming nascent Christian communities."],[2302.99,"And then we also now have these people who are not Jewish, who are familiar with the Jewish faith, but who are accepting Jesus as their Lord."],[2314.67,"So now the question that's going to come about is how does that community of these Greeks who have accepted Jesus relate to that part of the Jewish community that has accepted Jesus?"],[2331.91,"And that's complicated by the fact that there's also, of course, this big element of the Jewish community that has not accepted Jesus."],[2338.87,"So you've got Jews who have accepted Jesus trying to relate to Jews who have not accepted Jesus."],[2344.79,"And now you've also got these Gentiles who have accepted Jesus as a things came to the ears of the church in Jerusalem and they sent out Barnabas to go as far as Antioch."],[2362.99,"'ve d We we've seen met Barnabas before."],[2367.07,"Barnabas remember had some estates in uh Cyprus that he sold and uh to remain in Jerusalem."],[2375.95,"Uh it it's not directly said, it seems like he must have converted on the day of Pentecost, maybe shortly thereafter, when he was in Jerusalem, and rather than returning back to Cyprus, he sold his estates there and remained in Jerusalem and gave that money to the community."],[2395.87,"This is why they s would send him."],[2398.16,"Because he's part of this culture of these people who are doing this."],[2402.88,"So when they want to find out what's going on, they send someone who's gonna be able to relate to what's going on."],[2411.48,"I remember Barnabas, we think of Barnabas as as a name, just one name, but Bar Nabas in Aramaic, Bar means son of."],[2423.24,"So this is the son of Nabas."],[2425.48,"Nabas means encouragement or comfort."],[2429.72,"So it's possible that he was given the name Bar Nabas."],[2434.2,"Son of sort of meant a person who has this quality."],[2439.88,"So that may have been given to him as a name, or his father may have been named Nabas."],[2446.28,"It's not completely clear."],[2452.96,"And when he came to Antioch and had seen the grace of God, he was glad and encouraged them all that with purpose of heart they should continue with the Lord."],[2462.96,"So he sees what's going on."],[2464.96,"He sees that this is being blessed by God, and so he's all in favor of it."],[2469.92,"He encourages them to continue."],[2474.24,"Notice he says, continue with the Lord."],[2477.92,"This is we mentioned before that that this is true in St."],[2483.68,"Luke's gospel as well as in Acts."],[2486.8,"It's not sort of theological in the sense that that Saint Luke uh comes and tells you, okay, here's Jesus is is the Son of God, and da da da da."],[2496.46,"And it runs through his theology."],[2498.66,"But you sort of pick these things up in glancing ways by the way he phrases things and the way he talks."],[2500.42,"Like we talked about with brethren earlier"],[2507.64,"This is another one of those."],[2509.57,"That that to be to be a follower of Christ is to continue with the Lord, implying that Christ, the Lord, is there with them."],[2520.75,"And this is, of course, theologically what we express in the liturgy when we say Christ is in our midst."],[2526.69,"He is never shall be."],[2528.21,"Christ promised wherever two three are gathered in my name, I will be there in the midst of them."],[2533.09,"And so when it's he says he encouraged them to continue with the Lord, he's encouraging them to continue in their worship and their growth and their and their following Christ."],[2543.33,"Continue And this again implies a process rather than an end."],[2549.47,"Continue with what they're doing."],[2551.15,"Not, all right, you've done it."],[2556.99,"For he was a good man, full of the Holy Spirit and of faith, and a great many people were added to the Lord."],[2562.67,"Okay, so Barnabas doesn't just sort of show up and rubber stamp it, but he shows up and participates."],[2569.57,"And through his preaching and his teaching, even more people come in and become part of this community."],[2576.85,"Then Barnabas departed for Tarsus to seek Saul."],[2580.21,"And when he had found him, he brought him to Antioch."],[2583.57,"So it was that for a whole year they assembled with the church and taught it, taught a great many people."],[2587.01,"And the disciples were first called Christians in Antioch"],[2594.73,"So Barnabas said, remember, Barnabas was the one who sort of stuck up for Saul when Saul, St."],[2604.57,"Paul, first came back from Damascus to Jerusalem, and sort of came to the disciples are there and said, Oh no, no, don't worry, guys, I'm a Christian now."],[2612.65,"And everyone was sort of like, uh, you know, last week you were like killing Christians."],[2619.77,"Uh and uh Barnabas was the one who kind of stepped forward and and vouched for him."],[2626.01,"And so now, as he's beginning this ministry in Antioch, he says, you know, who would be a good person right to assist me with this?"],[2632.01,"He goes, he finds Saul, and he brings him back there"],[2637.89,"The two of them spend a year there preaching and teaching."],[2641.41,"Is there any particular significance to the fact that Paul has gone back to Tarsus instead of staying in Jerusalem?"],[2648.13,"Well, we're gonna find out later in one of his epistles that he actually went to Arabia for a while."],[2653.49,"Okay. That's before going back to Tarsus."],[2657.89,"Um and the point that St."],[2660.45,"Paul makes there is that uh that his apostleship and the gospel he received, he received directly from Christ."],[2670.53,"That he didn't sort of go to Jerusalem and say, okay, where do I sign up to do my Master of Divinity with Saint Peter and St."],[2680.09,"James. Where they can teach me and explain these things to me."],[2683.54,"Because I don't know."],[2685.2,"But that he went into he went into Arabia sort of on this spiritual retreat, uh, seemingly by himself."],[2694.48,"Um we'll talk about this more when we get into certain parts of St."],[2698.24,"Paul's epistles, but uh Saint Paul uh the idea that we in modern American culture send tend to have of Saint Paul, that he's sort of this bookish rabbi with thick glasses who you know sort of sits and reads the tribe, isn't really the way Saint Paul comes across when you read his epistles."],[2721.16,"Saint Paul, for one thing, was very clearly a mystic."],[2725.56,"He describes mystical experiences he had, not just the one on the road to Damascus, though that is one."],[2732.18,"Of seeing the Lord."],[2743.62,"You know, Hasidic Judaism or Orthodox rabbinical Judaism that we see today, and project that back into the first century."],[2752.34,"And that's not true, as we've seen already, and we'll continue to see."],[2755.78,"Um, we tend to project what a what a rabbi looks like now back to St"],[2761.38,"Paul because he was a teacher or a rabbi then."],[2764.54,"Um but it was it was a very a very different thing."],[2767.82,"He he was um I would go so far as to argue that Saint Paul, if you're gonna compare him to someone in the world today, he was a lot more like a monastic than he was like a contemporary rabbi."],[2782.3,"Uh-huh. He certainly spent a lot of time in reading and spiritual reading and spiritual retreat."],[2787.98,"He also spent a great deal of time in prayer and mystical prayer at that."],[2794.14,"Um so that's much more."],[2797.5,"He he uh uh he was much more like uh Saint Gregory Palamas than he was like Maimonides."],[2804.7,"Uh-huh. Um so yeah, he's he's a very different sort of figure."],[2810.31,"So he rather than sort of going there and being an apprentice to Saint Peter or Saint James or someone in Jerusalem, he goes on this sort of spiritual retreat to sort of further understand, you know, what it is that that God has called him to."],[2829.91,"Um and then uh now it it doesn't actually say that he was in Tarsus."],[2837.59,"It says that Barnabas say Barnabas went to Tarsus looking for him because he didn't know where he was."],[2845.4,"And we're told that he found him."],[2847.33,"Right Right. But we're not told that he found him in Tarsus, uh huh."],[2851.45,"So he may have just, you know, tracked him down."],[2853.77,"know. You But uh but yeah, that's that's sort of where he was by his own account, his own account later."],[2864.61,"But so now this is where St."],[2866.45,"Paul's ministry really, his public ministry where he's teaching and preaching Christianity, as we hear that last verse, but it's now being called Christianity."],[2881.01,"He's proclaiming and preaching that."],[2883.25,"And notice it's not in Antioch in the Jewish community or the Jewish Christian community in Antioch."],[2894.21,"At the beginning."],[2895.65,"It's to this group of converted Hellenists."],[2899.09,"Barnabas and St"],[2900.99,"That St. Paul are primarily preaching and ministering."],[2906.35,"Now we're gonna read some more here."],[2909.31,"But that's that's the primary initial focus of what he's doing at Antioch."],[2917.07,"Um and then we get this last note that the disciples, which is on the bottom of all of the letterhead in our Antiochan archdiocese, that the disciples were from first called Christians in Antioch."],[2929.15,"Um and Christian, essentially the word that's translated there as Christian means little Christ."],[2936.03,"Um, there's a lot of debate as to how we should understand that, whether it's just saying that these are little Christ, like they're like Christ."],[2943.31,"like. They're people who are Christ"],[2949.41,"Or um whether it's taking the actual definition of Christos, which means anointed one."],[2955.49,"Uh-huh. That they're called they're being called, but anointed one and they're being called anointed ones, uh huh."],[2962.87,"Referring to the Holy Spirit, uh-huh, or or baptism."],[2967.93,"Um so that's slightly unclear."],[2971.13,"I mean, I think both are theologically correct."],[2975.46,"These are people who have been anointed with and received the Holy Spirit."],[2979.3,"So that's true."],[2980.82,"Um, it's also true that if they are following Christ, they're seeking to become like Christ."],[2987.12,"So, you know, both of those meanings can be true, but historically, what was originally meant is not entirely clear."],[2995.52,"It's also interesting that it says it's passive."],[2998.72,"They were first called Christians in Antioch, which to me it's not necessarily what it says, but to me it always seemed to mean that outsiders called them that."],[3012.86,"They were called that."],[3014.14,"Yeah. Yes. Yeah."],[3015.9,"By and we don't know whether it was by the Gentiles at large, whether it was by the Jewish Christians, whether it was by the Jewish non-Christians, some combination of that."],[3028.38,"We don't know if it was meant to be a pejorative."],[3032.58,"Or whether it was meant just to be a description."],[3035.38,"Uh-huh. It's unclear."],[3037.54,"But at any rate, that that word is taken, obviously."],[3041.22,"You know, and used, you know, as an identifier."],[3051.14,"And and, you know, it it even as I've been doing this and I've been trying to sort of avoid it, you know, you kind of need such a term."],[3061.76,"Now we we we read already that they were referring to what we would call Christianity."],[3066.9,"Following Jesus Christ as the way."],[3069.75,"Uh but even when I'm trying to lay out these groups, I end up saying Jewish Christians."],[3074.07,"Uh-huh You know, and Gentile Christians and then Jewish non-Christians."],[3081.81,"You need, you know, and so that term, even if it might have originally been meant as a pejorative or a put down, uh has this obvious usefulness."],[3094.53,"And and it has this usefulness in showing what unites this Gentile community and this Jewish community who have both followed Christ."],[3104.03,"They now have this in common."],[3105.73,"They're Jewish Christians and Gentile Christians."],[3108.61,"And that separates them from Gentile non-Cistihrans and Jewish non-Christians."],[3113.65,"Listen next time as Father Stephen DeYoung continues his study of the the scriptures on Whole Counsel of God."],[3120.97,"Father Stephen's email address is wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."],[3126.33,"That's wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."]],"o":[{"t":25.84,"lv":2,"h":"Recap: Acts so far","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Starting <b>Acts 11</b>; first Acts study (intro to the book) is posted online</li><li class=\"d0\">Peter will <b>recap chapter 10</b> as we read</li><li class=\"d0\">Acts opening: Luke retells the <b>Ascension</b>; <b>Holy Spirit</b> comes on disciples in Jerusalem</li><li class=\"d1\">apostles' preaching, conversions, beginnings of the church</li><li class=\"d1\">→ death of <b>St. Stephen</b>, first martyr (relics found today, many centuries ago)</li><li class=\"d1\">at hands of a mob incited by religious leaders, incl. <b>Saul of Tarsus</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Persecution <b>scattered</b> Christians → meant to eliminate, ended up <b>spreading</b> it</li><li class=\"d1\">communities in <b>Damascus</b>, <b>Samaria</b>, the <b>Ethiopian</b> eunuch, and <b>Antioch</b> (ch. 11)</li><li class=\"d0\">Saul's repentance and calling on the road to Damascus - story now back with <b>Peter</b></li></ul>"},{"t":224.88,"lv":3,"h":"Cornelius recap","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Cornelius</b> the Roman centurion, friend of the Jewish community</li><li class=\"d1\">stopped worshiping other gods - only the <b>God of Israel</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>God-fearers</b>: had Jewish scriptures in Greek, gave to synagogues, personal piety</li><li class=\"d1\">not ready to go all the way: <b>circumcision</b>, food regulations, etc.</li><li class=\"d1\">a centurion would have to <b>give up his position</b> → stayed at the fringe while his career lasted</li><li class=\"d0\">Vision in prayer: call for <b>Peter</b> in Joppa</li><li class=\"d0\">Peter bears witness: <b>Holy Spirit</b> fell on Cornelius' household while still <b>Gentiles</b></li><li class=\"d1\">no circumcision, no ritual washings → Peter <b>baptized</b> them - \"what will soon be called Christians\"</li></ul>"},{"t":380.38,"lv":3,"h":"Q: first Gentile?","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: is this the <b>first Gentile</b>?</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Ethiopian eunuch</b> was baptized - Gentile</li><li class=\"d1\">but a community of <b>Ethiopian Jews</b> already existed → \"another step removed\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Romans = \"<b>Gentiles of all Gentiles</b>\" - actively oppressing Judeans</li><li class=\"d1\">this one even in <b>military/government</b> service</li><li class=\"d1\">\"most extreme case\" - as far outside their normal ideas as possible</li><li class=\"d0\">Peter returns to <b>Jerusalem</b>; not everyone agrees with what he did</li></ul>"},{"t":461.3,"lv":2,"h":"Acts 11:1 - Apostles and brethren","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"Apostles and brethren in Judea heard the Gentiles had also received the <b>Word of God</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Apostles</b> = leaders; <b>brethren</b> = rest of the community</li><li class=\"d1\">not just men - in Hebrew, Aramaic, Greek <b>mixed group = masculine</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Familial terms</b> already: members call each other <b>brothers and sisters</b></li><li class=\"d1\">later epistles (<b>1 Corinthians</b>): leaders = <b>fathers</b>, members = <b>children</b></li><li class=\"d1\">church = <b>household of God</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Protestant friends dislike clergy called \"<b>father</b>\" - \"this is where that comes from\"</li><li class=\"d1\">family: leaders like fathers, people like children, to each other <b>brothers and sisters in the Lord</b></li></ul>"},{"t":618.49,"lv":3,"h":"\"Word of God\" = Christ","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"Word of God\" ≠ the Bible</li><li class=\"d1\">Peter didn't give Cornelius a Bible or a copy of the Hebrew scriptures</li><li class=\"d1\">= <b>Christ and the gospel</b> - \"the gospel is Christ himself\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Gentiles have received <b>Christ</b>, not just Jewish people</li><li class=\"d0\">We <b>underestimate</b> how big this was</li><li class=\"d1\">not long ago even in this country, questions whether certain ethnicities could <b>receive Christ</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not absurd then: Jesus = the <b>Jewish Messiah</b>, king of the Jews; promises made to Jews, \"<b>none to the Romans</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Luke has been showing the <b>reversal of Babel</b> (Pentecost) - God is God of the <b>whole world</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not apparent to people then → response isn't just rejoicing: \"<b>Why? How does this work?</b>\"</li></ul>"},{"t":770.41,"lv":2,"h":"Acts 11:2-3 - Why eat with them?","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Peter up in Jerusalem: <b>circumcision party</b> contended</li><li class=\"d1\">\"You went into <b>uncircumcised men</b> and <b>ate with them</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">not even at baptism - \"why did you go into that house? why sit and eat?\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Sounds \"super racist\" to us - but <b>hospitality</b> weighed more in the ancient world</li><li class=\"d1\">US: families don't even eat dinner together half the time</li><li class=\"d0\">Being a guest, sitting at <b>table</b> = <b>fellowship and communion</b>, uniting yourself</li><li class=\"d1\">host or guest of a problem person = <b>joining yourself</b> to him</li><li class=\"d1\">why <b>Jesus</b> got heat from Pharisees for eating with tax collectors and sinners</li><li class=\"d1\">not \"they're filthy\" but \"how can you have <b>fellowship</b> with this person?\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Analogy: photo of someone chatting with <b>Hitler</b> - \"does that mean you agree with him?\"</li></ul>"},{"t":928.33,"lv":3,"h":"Not racism - table fellowship","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Not just \"racist against Gentiles, think they're filthy\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Torah</b>: nothing saying Gentiles <b>in themselves unclean</b></li><li class=\"d1\">they could make themselves unclean: <b>idol's temple</b>, pagan ritual</li><li class=\"d1\">just being a Gentile doesn't</li><li class=\"d0\">Issue: Peter sat and ate with a <b>pagan Roman centurion</b> as an <b>equal</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Where it's preserved today: the <b>Eucharist</b></li><li class=\"d1\">we don't give communion to \"just anybody off the street\"</li><li class=\"d1\">sharing the <b>chalice</b> = <b>one faith, one belief</b>, united in Christ</li><li class=\"d1\">so they see eating together as a <b>statement</b>: \"you and he are in it together\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1021.79,"lv":2,"h":"Acts 11:4-10 - Peter retells the vision","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Peter explained <b>in order from the beginning</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Joppa, praying, <b>trance</b>: object like a <b>great sheet</b> let down by <b>four corners</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"upside-down <b>parachute</b>\" with ropes</li><li class=\"d1\">contents: four-footed animals, wild beasts, <b>creeping things</b> (<i>herpeton</i> = lizards), birds</li><li class=\"d1\">= all the <b>unclean animals</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Voice: \"Rise, Peter, <b>kill and eat</b>\" - \"cook isn't in there anywhere\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Peter: \"Not so, Lord\" - nothing common or unclean has entered my mouth</li><li class=\"d0\">Voice: \"<b>What God has cleansed, you must not call common</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">not \"don't call these unclean\" - they <b>were</b> unclean at one point; <b>now I have cleansed them</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Done <b>three times</b>, all drawn back up to heaven</li></ul>"},{"t":1122.8,"lv":3,"h":"Q: what has God cleansed? The camp and the world","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: only these creatures, or <b>creation</b> now cleansed by Christ? → <b>the latter</b></li><li class=\"d0\">(After Revelation he'll loop back and do the <b>Pentateuch</b> again)</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Leviticus</b> food and cleanliness laws: wilderness <b>camp</b></li><li class=\"d1\">center = <b>tabernacle</b> where God lives</li><li class=\"d1\">outside = world <b>corrupted by sin</b>, under evil spiritual forces</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Leviticus 16</b>, Day of Atonement: two goats; Aaron lays hands, sins on one → sent out to <b>Azazel</b>, a demonic spirit \"in control of the world\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Camp kept <b>clean, pure, holy</b> so God can dwell there</li><li class=\"d1\">true of the <b>land</b> later; <b>temple</b> in the midst of the land</li><li class=\"d1\">when they become corrupt: <b>God leaves</b> the temple → destroyed - \"he can't be where sin is\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Food laws: allowed = <b>domesticated animals</b> of the camp</li><li class=\"d1\">not allowed: wild animals, found dead, <b>carrion birds</b></li><li class=\"d1\">kill a certain way, <b>drain the blood</b>, offer a portion to God, eat with family</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>halal</b> rules based on the Jewish ones - why no pork either</li><li class=\"d0\">All aimed at preserving <b>holy space</b> in a world under <b>dark powers</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1322.93,"lv":3,"h":"Christ cleanses the whole world","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Crucifixion/resurrection cleanses not just Judea but the <b>whole world</b></li><li class=\"d1\">defeats the <b>dark spiritual forces</b> → no longer in control</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Christ is Lord of all the earth</b></li><li class=\"d0\">→ animals now under <b>Christ's dominion</b></li><li class=\"d1\">more importantly: <b>all peoples</b> no longer under dark forces, under <b>Christ's lordship</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not just Israel as \"little camp in the midst of the world\" = what the vision aims at</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Paul</b> develops it in epistles</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Colossians</b>: formerly enslaved by <b>elemental powers</b>, worshiping gods</li><li class=\"d1\">quotes Psalm: \"<b>all the gods of the nations are demons</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">doesn't say they don't exist - <b>evil forces</b> who enslaved you, led you to destruction, not worthy of being called gods</li><li class=\"d1\">Christ <b>set you free</b> by defeating them</li></ul>"},{"t":1439.91,"lv":3,"h":"\"Satan is lord of this world\" - no longer","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Phrase \"Satan is the <b>Lord of this world</b>\": <b>ended with Christ</b> - was true, isn't now</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>John</b>, final discourse: \"now is the <b>prince of this world judged</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Revelation</b>: <b>binding of Satan</b>, thrown in a pit</li><li class=\"d1\">Christ reigns with the saints for a long period - \"<b>thousand years</b>\" metaphorical</li><li class=\"d1\">end: devil loosed for a last <b>frenzied attack</b> → ultimate defeat</li><li class=\"d0\">Reason for the span: as many as possible to <b>repent</b></li><li class=\"d1\">God in his <b>mercy</b> has extended it</li><li class=\"d0\">So \"what God has cleansed\" = animals brought into God's <b>dominion, reign</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1549.58,"lv":3,"h":"<i>Basileia</i> = reign, not a place","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Translate <i>basileia</i> as \"kingdom\" - makes us think of a <b>place</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"blessed is the kingdom\" in liturgy; \"kingdom come\" as if somewhere else, will show up</li><li class=\"d0\">Really = <b>dominion, rule, reign</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not a physical kingdom on earth like the OT</li><li class=\"d1\">= <b>Christ's rule</b> over all the earth</li><li class=\"d0\">We bless it and <b>enter it</b>, under his lordship, when we gather in <b>worship</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Gospel spreading = extends <b>Christ's lordship</b> to other places and people</li></ul>"},{"t":1625.35,"lv":2,"h":"Acts 11:11-14 - Cornelius' angel; Peter's name","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Peter continues: three times, drawn up</li><li class=\"d0\">At that very moment <b>three men</b> stood at the house, sent from <b>Caesarea</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Spirit: go with them, <b>doubting nothing</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>six brethren</b> accompanied him into the man's house</li><li class=\"d0\">Cornelius told of angel: \"send to Joppa for Simon <b>surnamed Peter</b>, who will tell you words by which you and <b>all your household will be saved</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Surname\" misleading - not first/last name</li><li class=\"d1\"><i>Petros</i> = <b>nickname</b> from Jesus: rock, \"<b>Rocky</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">fits his character - \"rough and tumble guy\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Peter's point: <b>two visions</b>, and the men arrived <b>just as he was having his</b></li><li class=\"d1\">→ clearly <b>something God wanted</b>, at this time, with these people</li></ul>"},{"t":1744.77,"lv":2,"h":"Acts 11:15-17 - The Spirit fell","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"As I began to speak, the <b>Holy Spirit fell</b> upon them, as upon us <b>at the beginning</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">remembered the Lord: <b>John baptized with water</b>, you'll be baptized with the <b>Spirit</b></li><li class=\"d0\">\"God gave them the <b>same gift</b>... who was I that I could <b>withstand God</b>?\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Order reversed from the usual in Acts (<b>baptism then Spirit</b>)</li><li class=\"d1\">God didn't wait - Spirit comes while still preaching</li><li class=\"d1\">why: a <b>signal</b> - \"you need to baptize these people, don't hold off\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Peter: if they've already received the Spirit, how tell them <b>no water</b>?</li></ul>"},{"t":1807.25,"lv":2,"h":"Acts 11:18 - Repentance to life","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">They <b>became silent</b> and <b>glorified God</b>: \"God has also granted the Gentiles <b>repentance to life</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">A \"very good group\" of Jewish Christians</li><li class=\"d1\">later in the NT: other groups <b>not so understanding</b> about Gentiles</li><li class=\"d1\">these \"come around fairly quickly\"</li><li class=\"d0\">To deny it you'd have to call <b>Peter a liar</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Notice how they say it - not \"nice that Gentiles can get saved too\"</li><li class=\"d1\">baptism/Spirit = <b>beginning</b>, not the end</li><li class=\"d1\">beginning of a <b>life of repentance</b> → leads to <b>eternal life</b></li><li class=\"d1\">gift = <b>repentance and forgiveness of sins</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Gives Jewish Christians a way to understand it</li><li class=\"d1\">their objection: Gentiles' <b>idolatry, sexual immorality</b></li><li class=\"d1\">if God granted repentance → \"<b>that's not who they are anymore</b>\" - new identity in Christ</li></ul>"},{"t":1960.53,"lv":2,"h":"Acts 11:19 - Scattered by persecution","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Those <b>scattered after Stephen</b> went to <b>Phoenicia, Cyprus, Antioch</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"preaching the word to <b>no one but the Jews</b> only\"</li><li class=\"d1\">they hadn't had Peter's vision</li><li class=\"d1\">telling Jewish communities: Messiah has come, <b>Jesus of Nazareth</b></li><li class=\"d1\">forming more <b>nascent Christian communities</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Phoenicia</b> = give or take modern <b>Lebanon</b>; <b>Cyprus</b> still Cyprus</li></ul>"},{"t":2007.37,"lv":3,"h":"Which Antioch?","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Many cities named <b>Antioch</b> - e.g. <b>Pisidian Antioch</b>, several in Acts</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Antiochus</b> = family name of Greek rulers of the <b>Syrian</b> kingdom</li><li class=\"d1\">named cities after themselves, fathers, sons</li><li class=\"d1\">same as many <b>Caesareas</b>, <b>Alexandrias</b> (after Alexander the Great)</li><li class=\"d0\">This one: <b>capital of the East</b>, on the <b>Orontes</b> River</li><li class=\"d1\">capital of <b>Syria</b>; today in <b>Turkey</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"historically\" much of Lebanon was in Syria too</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Third biggest city</b> of the Roman Empire at this point</li></ul>"},{"t":2089.21,"lv":3,"h":"Antioch's Jewish community","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Second-largest <b>Jewish community</b> after Jerusalem</li><li class=\"d1\">at founding about <b>a third Jewish</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Jewish district: the <b>Kerateion</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Titus</b> destroying the Jerusalem temple took the decorative <b>cherubim</b>, hung them in the Jewish quarter of Antioch</li><li class=\"d1\">message: \"<b>we could wipe this out too</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Community lasted to <b>St. John Chrysostom</b></li><li class=\"d1\">occasioned his <b>sermons against the Judaizers</b> (\"against the Jews\")</li><li class=\"d1\">conflicts between Jewish and Christian communities</li><li class=\"d1\">late 4th/early 5th c.: Jewish community <b>bigger than the Christian</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Why Antioch named as a city, others only regions: so big it's <b>noteworthy</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2187.69,"lv":2,"h":"Acts 11:20-21 - Preaching to the Hellenists","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Some <b>men of Cyprus and Cyrene</b> came to Antioch, spoke to the <b>Hellenists</b>, preaching the <b>Lord Jesus</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Cyrene</b> = North Africa</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Greek</b> would be their primary language even though Jewish - far from Palestine</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Hellenists</b> here ≠ Hellenistic Jews</li><li class=\"d1\">context: \"only to the Jews, but some went to the Hellenists\" - would make no sense otherwise</li><li class=\"d1\">= <b>Greeks who are God-fearers</b>, like Cornelius</li><li class=\"d1\">fluent in Greek, acculturated → go to Greeks <b>near Judaism</b> as well as Jewish communities</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Hand of the Lord was with them\": <b>great number believed</b> and turned to the Lord</li></ul>"},{"t":2288.73,"lv":3,"h":"Three groups to relate","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Beyond Cornelius' one family:</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Jewish</b> communities accepting Jesus as Messiah → Christian communities</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>non-Jews</b> familiar with the faith accepting Jesus as <b>Lord</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Question: how do the <b>Greek believers</b> relate to the <b>Jewish believers</b>?</li><li class=\"d1\">complicated by the big element of <b>Jews who haven't accepted Jesus</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Jews who did, relating to Jews who didn't - plus now <b>Gentiles</b> who did</li></ul>"},{"t":2344.79,"lv":2,"h":"Acts 11:22-24 - Barnabas sent to Antioch","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">News reaches <b>Jerusalem</b>; they send <b>Barnabas</b> as far as Antioch</li><li class=\"d1\">sold his <b>estates in Cyprus</b>, stayed in Jerusalem, gave money to community</li><li class=\"d1\">probably converted at <b>Pentecost</b> or soon after</li><li class=\"d1\">sent because he's from <b>this culture</b> - can relate</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Bar-Nabas</b>: <i>bar</i> = <b>son of</b>; <i>Nabas</i> = <b>encouragement/comfort</b></li><li class=\"d1\">perhaps given as a name meaning a person with that quality; or father's name - \"not completely clear\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Saw the <b>grace of God</b>, glad, <b>encouraged them all</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"purpose of heart\" continue with the Lord</li><li class=\"d1\">seeing it blessed, \"all in favor\"</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Good man, full of the <b>Holy Spirit and faith</b>\" - great many <b>added to the Lord</b></li><li class=\"d1\">doesn't \"rubber stamp\" - participates, preaching, teaching</li></ul>"},{"t":2474.24,"lv":3,"h":"\"Continue with the Lord\"","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Luke's theology comes in <b>glancing ways</b>, by phrasing - not stated outright</li><li class=\"d1\">like \"brethren\" earlier</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Follower of Christ = continue with the Lord</b></li><li class=\"d1\">implies Christ is <b>there with them</b></li><li class=\"d1\">liturgy: \"<b>Christ is in our midst</b>\" - \"wherever two or three are gathered... I will be in the midst\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Encouraged to continue in <b>worship, growth, following Christ</b></li><li class=\"d1\">implies a <b>process</b> rather than an end - \"not, all right, you've done it\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2576.85,"lv":2,"h":"Acts 11:25-26a - Barnabas fetches Saul","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Barnabas departs for <b>Tarsus</b> to seek <b>Saul</b>, brings him to Antioch</li><li class=\"d1\">a <b>whole year</b> assembled with the church, taught many</li><li class=\"d0\">Barnabas had <b>vouched for Saul</b> when he first came from Damascus to Jerusalem</li><li class=\"d1\">disciples: \"last week you were killing Christians\"</li><li class=\"d1\">now asks \"who'd be a good person to assist me?\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2641.41,"lv":3,"h":"Q: Paul in Tarsus, and Arabia","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: significance of Paul going back to <b>Tarsus</b>?</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul says later (an epistle) he went to <b>Arabia</b> first</li><li class=\"d1\">point: his <b>apostleship and gospel</b> came <b>directly from Christ</b></li><li class=\"d1\">didn't go to Jerusalem to do a \"<b>Master of Divinity</b> with Peter and James\"</li><li class=\"d1\">went on <b>spiritual retreat</b>, seemingly alone</li><li class=\"d0\">Text doesn't say Barnabas found him <b>in Tarsus</b></li><li class=\"d1\">went looking because he didn't know where he was; \"tracked him down\"</li><li class=\"d1\">where he was \"by his own account\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2694.48,"lv":3,"h":"Paul as mystic, not bookish rabbi","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Modern picture: \"<b>bookish rabbi</b> with thick glasses\" - not how Paul comes across in epistles</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul = clearly a <b>mystic</b></li><li class=\"d1\">describes <b>mystical experiences</b> - not just <b>Damascus road</b> (seeing the Lord)</li><li class=\"d0\">We project <b>Hasidic/Orthodox rabbinical Judaism</b> back into the first century - wrong</li><li class=\"d0\">Closer to a <b>monastic</b> than a contemporary rabbi</li><li class=\"d1\">spiritual reading, retreat, much <b>mystical prayer</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"more like <b>St. Gregory Palamas</b> than like <b>Maimonides</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Went on retreat to understand what God had <b>called him to</b>, not apprenticing to Peter or James</li></ul>"},{"t":2864.61,"lv":3,"h":"Paul's public ministry begins","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Here Paul's <b>public ministry</b> of teaching and preaching starts</li><li class=\"d1\">now called <b>Christianity</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Notice: not to the Jewish community/Jewish Christians in Antioch at first</li><li class=\"d1\">to the <b>converted Hellenists</b> - Barnabas and Paul's primary initial focus</li><li class=\"d0\">More to read, but that's the primary initial focus at Antioch</li></ul>"},{"t":2917.07,"lv":2,"h":"Acts 11:26b - First called Christians","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"Disciples first called <b>Christians</b> in Antioch\" - on the letterhead of the <b>Antiochian Archdiocese</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><i>Christianos</i> = \"<b>little Christ</b>\" - debated meaning</li><li class=\"d1\">\"like Christ\"?</li><li class=\"d1\">or <i>Christos</i> = <b>anointed one</b> → \"anointed ones\"</li><li class=\"d1\">referring to <b>Holy Spirit</b> or <b>baptism</b></li><li class=\"d1\">both theologically correct: <b>anointed</b> with Spirit; following Christ → <b>seeking to become like him</b></li><li class=\"d1\">original meaning \"not entirely clear\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2995.52,"lv":3,"h":"Who called them that? Why the term is useful","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Passive</b>: \"were called\" → seems <b>outsiders</b> called them that</li><li class=\"d1\">unknown: Gentiles at large, Jewish Christians, <b>Jewish non-Christians</b>, a mix</li><li class=\"d1\">maybe <b>pejorative</b>, maybe just description</li><li class=\"d0\">Word taken and used as <b>identifier</b></li><li class=\"d1\">he himself needs it: \"Jewish Christians, Gentile Christians, Jewish non-Christians\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Shows what <b>unites</b> Gentile and Jewish communities who follow Christ</li><li class=\"d1\">= <b>Jewish Christians and Gentile Christians</b></li><li class=\"d1\">separates them from <b>Gentile non-Christians and Jewish non-Christians</b></li></ul>"}]});
